1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,079
You've written pieces of this document across the last six lectures: a threshold

2
00:00:05,079 --> 00:00:09,453
sentence, a dataset commitment, a rubric commitment, a failure taxonomy, a

3
00:00:09,453 --> 00:00:12,172
degradation path, a human-in-the-loop pattern.

4
00:00:12,172 --> 00:00:17,019
Here they all are, assembled into one template, in the order they actually belong.

5
00:00:17,019 --> 00:00:19,265
This is A01, attached to this lecture.

6
00:00:19,265 --> 00:00:24,230
Download it and follow along, because the fastest way to learn a template is to fill

7
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:28,072
one in while someone walks through it, not to read it cold later.

8
00:00:28,072 --> 00:00:30,673
Nothing in this template is new information.

9
00:00:30,673 --> 00:00:35,579
Every sentence you're about to see was earned, specifically, in one of the last six

10
00:00:35,579 --> 00:00:36,110
lectures.

11
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:40,957
What's new is seeing all of it in one place, in the order an engineer or a QA lead

12
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:45,745
would actually want to read it, instead of scattered across six separate insights

13
00:00:45,745 --> 00:00:48,287
you'd have to remember to combine yourself.

14
00:00:48,287 --> 00:00:51,597
That ordering matters more than it looks like it should.

15
00:00:51,597 --> 00:00:56,503
A reviewer reading a PRD top to bottom builds trust or loses it section by section.

16
00:00:56,503 --> 00:01:01,409
Open with a sharp threshold and a real dataset commitment, and the failure taxonomy

17
00:01:01,409 --> 00:01:04,955
a few sections later reads as thorough rather than paranoid.

18
00:01:04,955 --> 00:01:09,743
Open with vague acceptance criteria, and nothing after it gets read as carefully,

19
00:01:09,743 --> 00:01:13,703
because the reviewer has already learned not to trust the document.

20
00:01:14,253 --> 00:01:19,088
Read straight down that left column and notice something: the first two rows are

21
00:01:19,088 --> 00:01:21,565
close to what any decent PRD already has.

22
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:25,735
Everything from row three onward is what this section actually added.

23
00:01:25,735 --> 00:01:30,388
That's deliberate, and it's worth saying plainly before you fill in your own.

24
00:01:30,388 --> 00:01:34,376
You're not throwing out what you already know about writing a PRD.

25
00:01:34,376 --> 00:01:39,271
Problem, context, archetype, all of that stays exactly as familiar as it's always

26
00:01:39,271 --> 00:01:39,573
been.

27
00:01:39,573 --> 00:01:44,166
What's new is everything underneath it: the six sections that turn a hopeful

28
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:49,121
description into something QA, engineering, and a launch reviewer can all hold you

29
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:49,302
to.

30
00:01:49,302 --> 00:01:54,318
Walk the table again with one question in mind: which row would someone in a launch

31
00:01:54,318 --> 00:01:56,070
review actually push back on?

32
00:01:56,070 --> 00:02:00,300
Almost never the problem statement, everyone agreed on that weeks ago.

33
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:05,074
It's the threshold, the dataset, and the degradation path, the three rows teams

34
00:02:05,074 --> 00:02:10,090
historically leave vaguest, precisely because they used to be the hardest to write.

35
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:11,661
They're not hard any more.

36
00:02:11,661 --> 00:02:14,380
You've already written a version of each one.

37
00:02:14,930 --> 00:02:19,455
Keep that column in view as you fill in your own template, and use it the way it's

38
00:02:19,455 --> 00:02:23,869
meant: a pointer back to the lecture that has the full method, not a scoreboard.

39
00:02:24,419 --> 00:02:29,543
This split matters because a common reaction to this whole section is to assume it

40
00:02:29,543 --> 00:02:32,792
demands a completely different way of writing a PRD.

41
00:02:32,792 --> 00:02:33,480
It doesn't.

42
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:38,229
Everything in the first column is the PRD skill you already have, untouched.

43
00:02:38,229 --> 00:02:43,291
What's genuinely new lives entirely in the second column, and it's new because it

44
00:02:43,291 --> 00:02:48,353
answers questions a deterministic feature never had to ask: what happens across a

45
00:02:48,353 --> 00:02:53,540
range of outputs, not one, and what happens specifically when this one lands on the

46
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:55,039
wrong side of the range.

47
00:02:55,039 --> 00:02:59,789
Notice, too, that the second column is shorter to list than it was to learn.

48
00:02:59,789 --> 00:03:01,538
Three bullets, six lectures.

49
00:03:01,538 --> 00:03:06,225
That's not a coincidence, and it's not this lecture cutting corners either.

50
00:03:06,225 --> 00:03:11,100
Each of those three bullets is carrying an entire method behind it: a coverage

51
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:15,786
strategy, a calibration protocol, a taxonomy built from real failure modes.

52
00:03:15,786 --> 00:03:19,036
The template compresses the method into a checklist.

53
00:03:19,036 --> 00:03:22,348
It doesn't replace the method, and you now have both.

54
00:03:22,898 --> 00:03:27,642
Say the honest caveat, because a template can create a false sense of completeness

55
00:03:27,642 --> 00:03:28,510
all on its own.

56
00:03:28,510 --> 00:03:33,255
Write "the rubric is attached" in that section without an actual calibrated rubric

57
00:03:33,255 --> 00:03:37,825
behind it, and you've reproduced the exact failure this whole section opened by

58
00:03:37,825 --> 00:03:38,462
diagnosing.

59
00:03:38,462 --> 00:03:42,801
You've just hidden it one layer deeper, behind a section heading that looks

60
00:03:42,801 --> 00:03:43,322
thorough.

61
00:03:43,322 --> 00:03:45,926
The template is scaffolding for the thinking.

62
00:03:45,926 --> 00:03:48,182
It was never a substitute for doing it.

63
00:03:48,182 --> 00:03:52,984
A reviewer skimming eight completed sections will feel reassured whether or not the

64
00:03:52,984 --> 00:03:56,224
content underneath is real, and that's exactly the risk.

65
00:03:56,224 --> 00:03:59,407
The honest test isn't whether every box has text in it.

66
00:03:59,407 --> 00:04:04,035
It's whether someone could act on that text: run the calibration check, find the

67
00:04:04,035 --> 00:04:05,713
dataset, apply the threshold.

68
00:04:05,713 --> 00:04:10,284
If a section reads well but nobody could actually do anything with it, it's not

69
00:04:10,284 --> 00:04:12,193
finished, it's just well written.

70
00:04:12,743 --> 00:04:17,613
That's the instruction for the rest of today, not a suggestion for sometime this

71
00:04:17,613 --> 00:04:18,100
quarter.

72
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:23,092
Start with acceptance criteria specifically, because it's the section that exposes

73
00:04:23,092 --> 00:04:26,258
whether the rest of the document is doing real work.

74
00:04:26,258 --> 00:04:31,128
Once you've written a threshold you'd actually hold a launch to, the dataset and

75
00:04:31,128 --> 00:04:35,876
rubric sections stop being paperwork and start being the thing that makes that

76
00:04:35,876 --> 00:04:37,094
threshold checkable.

77
00:04:37,094 --> 00:04:42,086
Expect it to take longer than filling in the problem statement did, and treat that

78
00:04:42,086 --> 00:04:43,790
as a good sign, not a delay.

79
00:04:43,790 --> 00:04:47,260
The problem statement was never the hard part of any PRD.

80
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:50,426
It never has been, for any feature, AI or otherwise.

81
00:04:50,426 --> 00:04:55,296
The fact that acceptance criteria now takes real thought is this section working

82
00:04:55,296 --> 00:04:56,513
exactly as intended.

83
00:04:57,063 --> 00:04:58,841
One thing before you move on.

84
00:04:58,841 --> 00:05:01,783
Download the template, attached to this lecture.

85
00:05:01,783 --> 00:05:06,870
It's built with all eight sections in place, prompts included, so you're filling in

86
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:09,137
blanks, not staring at an empty page.

87
00:05:09,137 --> 00:05:14,040
Then write the acceptance criteria section, for real, for a feature you actually

88
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:15,205
have some stake in.

89
00:05:15,205 --> 00:05:16,063
Rough is fine.

90
00:05:16,063 --> 00:05:18,331
Blank is the only wrong answer today.

91
